DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       The  pptthhrreeaadd__sseettccaanncceellssttaattee() sets the cancelability state of the call‐
       ing thread to the value given in  _s_t_a_t_e.   The  previous  cancelability
       state  of  the thread is returned in the buffer pointed to by _o_l_d_s_t_a_t_e.
       The _s_t_a_t_e argument must have one of the following values:

       PPTTHHRREEAADD__CCAANNCCEELL__EENNAABBLLEE
              The thread is cancelable.  This  is  the  default  cancelability
              state  in  all  new  threads, including the initial thread.  The
              thread’s cancelability type determines when a cancelable  thread
              will respond to a cancelation request.

       PPTTHHRREEAADD__CCAANNCCEELL__DDIISSAABBLLEE
              The  thread  is not cancelable.  If a cancelation request is re‐
              ceived, it is blocked until cancelability is enabled.

       The pptthhrreeaadd__sseettccaanncceellttyyppee() sets the cancelability type of the  calling
       thread  to the value given in _t_y_p_e.  The previous cancelability type of
       the thread is returned in the buffer pointed to by _o_l_d_t_y_p_e.   The  _t_y_p_e
       argument must have one of the following values:

       PPTTHHRREEAADD__CCAANNCCEELL__DDEEFFEERRRREEDD
              A  cancelation request is deferred until the thread next calls a
              function that is a cancelation point (see pptthhrreeaaddss(7)).  This is
              the default cancelability type in all new threads, including the
              initial thread.

              Even with deferred cancelation, a cancelation point in an  asyn‐
              chronous  signal  handler may still be acted upon and the effect
              is as if it was an asynchronous cancelation.

       PPTTHHRREEAADD__CCAANNCCEELL__AASSYYNNCCHHRROONNOOUUSS
              The thread can be canceled at any time.  (Typically, it will  be
              canceled  immediately  upon receiving a cancelation request, but
              the system doesn’t guarantee this.)

       The set‐and‐get operation performed  by  each  of  these  functions  is
       atomic  with  respect  to other threads in the process calling the same
       function.

